---
/**
 * The audit request form. Posts to /api/contact.
 *
 * Rebuilt 1 Sep 2026 from frame 1a of `_docs/Automation Audit.dc.html`. It was
 * a general enquiry form ("Send"); the offer is now a free automation audit and
 * the form is the way into it. Five fields, four required.
 *
 * ⚠️ SHARED BY TWO PAGES. `/` renders it through `v2/ContactV2.astro` and
 * `/v1` through `ContactSection.astro`. There is deliberately one form and one
 * endpoint, because the alternative is two forms drifting against a single
 * `FIELDS` table — which is landmine 14 with extra steps. The visible
 * consequence is that /v1's contact section now shows the audit form. That is
 * accepted: /v1 is a record of a page's layout and copy, not of a form, it is
 * noindex, and nothing links to it. Fork this component only if the owner
 * wants /v1 frozen to the letter, and if you do, the old field set has to keep
 * working in contact.ts or /v1 starts dropping submissions silently.
 *
 * ⚠️ Field names here must match LIMITS and FIELDS in src/pages/api/contact.ts.
 * Adding or removing a field in one place and not the other collects the
 * answer and silently drops it. See CLAUDE.md landmine 14. This change removed
 * `website` from both files in the same commit.
 *
 * Native POST, no client bundle, works with JavaScript disabled.
 */
---

<form class="form" action="/api/contact" method="POST">
  {/* ⚠️ Honeypot, and note the name. Landmine 16.

      It must never share a name with a real field. It was `company_website`
      until 14 Aug 2026, when the copy began asking visitors for their company
      website as a genuine question; had both kept that name, every real
      submission would have tripped the bot check in contact.ts, been dropped
      without an email, and still shown the sender a success page.

      That collision is gone twice over now — the `website` field was removed
      entirely on 1 Sep 2026 — but the name stays `contact_fax` regardless. The
      trap is the label as much as the name: nothing on this site asks for a fax
      number, so no visitor will ever fill it in. If it ever changes it changes
      in contact.ts in the same commit.

      Positioned off-screen rather than display:none, which bots check for.
      Hidden from assistive tech and skipped by the tab order. */}
  <div class="hp" aria-hidden="true">
    <label for="f-contact-fax">Fax number</label>
    <input
      id="f-contact-fax"
      name="contact_fax"
      type="text"
      tabindex="-1"
      autocomplete="off"
    />
  </div>

  {/* ⚠️ Side by side, from the design. Both are short, and pairing them keeps
      the card from opening with two near-empty full-width rows before the
      questions that matter. They stack again below 640px — a 50% column on a
      phone is not a name field, it is a stub. */}
  <div class="row">
    <div>
      <label for="f-name">Name</label>
      <input id="f-name" name="name" type="text" autocomplete="name" required />
    </div>
    <div>
      <label for="f-email">Email</label>
      <input id="f-email" name="email" type="email" autocomplete="email" required />
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* Required as of the audit rebuild. The audit is written for a specific
      business, so a request without one cannot be answered. */}
  <label for="f-biz">Your business</label>
  <input
    id="f-biz"
    name="business"
    type="text"
    autocomplete="organization"
    placeholder="Kona HVAC, repair and install"
    required
  />

  {/* The one that matters, so it is the tall field and it sits above the
      optional one. This is the answer the whole audit is written from. */}
  <label for="f-timesink">What eats the most time in your week?</label>
  <textarea
    id="f-timesink"
    name="timesink"
    class="tall"
    placeholder="Tell me what you or your team do by hand every week. Retyping orders, chasing invoices, scheduling, follow-ups, whatever it is."
    required
  ></textarea>

  {/* ⚠️ A single-line input, not a textarea. It was a textarea when this asked
      "What software are you running?"; the design draws one line and the
      placeholder is three words long, so a five-row box invited an essay for
      the least important answer. The 5000 cap in contact.ts is unchanged — an
      input can still be pasted into. */}
  <label for="f-tools">
    What tools are you using now? <span class="opt">(optional)</span>
  </label>
  <input
    id="f-tools"
    name="tools"
    type="text"
    placeholder="Spreadsheets, QuickBooks, texts"
  />

  {/* "Request my audit", from the design. It was "Send", and "Show me your
      busywork" and "Book a call" before that. It still describes what pressing
      it does rather than what arrives afterwards, which is the rule this
      button has always followed — and unlike "Book a call" it is honest,
      because nothing here books anything.

      The arrow glyph came off: the design draws none, and it was there to make
      "Send" feel like an action. "Request my audit" does not need the help. */}
  <button type="submit" class="btn">Request my audit</button>

  {/* From the design. Answers the three things that stop someone pressing it:
      what it costs, what it commits them to, and what happens to their email.
      Replaces the reply-time promise, which now lives in the section copy and
      in the auto-reply. */}
  <p class="form-note">No cost. No obligation. I won&rsquo;t add you to any list.</p>
</form>

<style>
  .form {
    background: var(--color-white); border: 1px solid var(--color-ink);
    border-radius: 0; padding: 30px;
    max-width: 100%;
  }
  .hp {
    position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden; clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* Sentence case at nav size, not uppercase micro. These labels are
     questions, and a question set in 11px uppercase with .14em tracking reads
     as a system message rather than as something a person is asking.
     --color-body, not --color-faint: faint is 3.66:1 on the card ground and
     fails AA for text at this size. Matches the design exactly (#3B423D at
     12.5px/500). */
  .form label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-nav); font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-mono); color: var(--color-body);
    display: block; margin-bottom: 7px;
  }
  /* ⚠️ --color-quiet, not --color-faint. The design draws this at #767C76,
     which is --color-faint and fails AA at 3.66:1; the build brief calls for
     --color-quiet, which clears it at 5.49:1 and is a barely perceptible two
     steps darker. Following the brief here rather than the drawing, because
     the two disagree and only one of them passes. Raised with the owner. */
  .opt { color: var(--color-quiet); font-weight: 400; }

  /* 22px column gap, from the design. The fields keep their own 18px bottom
     margin, so no row gap is needed here. */
  .row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 22px; }

  .form input, .form textarea, .form select {
    width: 100%; padding: 11px 13px; border: 1px solid var(--color-ink);
    border-radius: 0; background: var(--color-paper);
    font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: var(--text-ui);
    color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  .form input:focus, .form textarea:focus, .form select:focus {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent-dark); outline-offset: -1px;
    border-color: var(--color-accent-dark);
  }
  .form textarea { resize: vertical; min-height: 82px; }
  /* The one tall field. 128px is four lines at --text-ui, which is enough to
     show that a paragraph is wanted without the box dominating the card. */
  .form textarea.tall { min-height: 128px; }
  /* 16px, not 17. --text-ui exists for this: 16 is the threshold at which iOS
     stops zooming the page when a field is focused, so this may not go lower.
     See the type scale in global.css. */

  .form .btn { width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; min-height: 50px; text-align: center; }

  /* Sits under the button, so it reads as a promise about what happens next
     rather than as a field hint. */
  .form-note {
    font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: var(--text-small); line-height: var(--leading-body);
    color: var(--color-faint); margin-top: 16px; text-align: center;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    /* Full width again. A 50% column on a phone is not a name field. */
    .row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0; }
    .form { padding: 22px 18px; }
    .form input, .form textarea, .form select { padding: 13px; margin-bottom: 16px; }
    .form label { font-size: 12px; }
    .form textarea { min-height: 76px; }
    .form textarea.tall { min-height: 118px; }
    .form-note { font-size: 14px; }
  }
</style>
